# The semantic index's three matrices are raw int8/uint16, and they are committed, because docs/ is the
# deployable artefact -- GitHub Pages serves this repository's bytes verbatim (`build_type: legacy`).
#
# Marked binary explicitly rather than left to Git's autodetection, which reads the first 8 KB and guesses
# "text" if it finds no NUL byte. Today every one of these files has NUL bytes early -- a component that
# quantised to zero -- so the guess is right and the committed blobs match the build byte for byte. That is
# a property of the *data*, not of the format. A rebuild against a different corpus, a different `dims`, or
# a smaller vocabulary could easily produce a first page with none, and on this Windows checkout
# `core.autocrlf` would then rewrite every 0x0D 0x0A pair in a matrix of signed bytes on the way in.
#
# The result would not look like corruption. The file lengths would still divide by `dims`, so the shape
# assertions in tests/semantic_test.py would pass; the fingerprint covers docs/data.json rather than these
# bytes, so the staleness guard would pass; and the page would load the index, report SEM_STATE "live", and
# rank every query against damaged vectors. Cheaper to state the format than to hope the data keeps hiding
# the hazard.
docs/search/*.bin binary

# Written with newline="\n" by scripts/27_semantic.py and committed as LF, so that the bytes a reader
# downloads do not depend on which OS ran the build. Pinned for the same reason: sorted keys and LF are
# what keep these two files out of every diff.
docs/search/*.json text eol=lf

# The vendored Traceway SDK and web-vitals builds are npm's bytes, unmodified, and
# tests/deeplinks_test.py pins their hashes to the published files. Neither has a CR in it today, so
# autocrlf would leave the committed blobs alone -- but on this Windows checkout it would write CRLF into
# the working tree, and a byte comparison against the npm tarball would then fail on a file nobody
# changed. Not text, so what is on disk, in the repository and on the site are the same bytes everywhere.
docs/assets/traceway-1.2.1.js -text
docs/assets/web-vitals-6.2.3.js -text
